WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.940
ALICIA SALMERON: My
name is Alicia Salmeron,

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.770
and I lead strategic
partnerships

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.020
at Google for Education.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.410
Today, you're going to hear from
students and researchers who

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.710
are using GCP to discover
black holes in the universe,

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.200
new painkillers that could help
us address the opioid crisis,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.220
as well as communication devices
that can enable the deaf.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.350
Joining me today are Alon and
Tomer from Columbia University,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.970
Vaikunth from Stanford SLAC,
and Nil from the University

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.460
of Southern California.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.830
You'll hear their
stories in just a moment.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:48.720
This quote from Ryan
Abernathey, a climate researcher

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.550
at Columbia University,
really says it all for me.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.240
The amount of data in academic
as well as scientific research

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.920
is exploding.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.690
And researchers
really have no option

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.760
but to look to the cloud.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.860
But what the cloud is
enabling them to do

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.400
is to make discoveries faster.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.040
Ryan used GCP in his lab.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.980
And he made note that
he's able to ask more

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.660
questions, different questions.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.342
Take a moment to
read that last line.

00:01:20.342 --> 00:01:21.550
Really hits home, doesn't it?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.880
We have two programs that enable
students and researchers to get

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.430
started on GCP at no cost.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.670
The first is the Education
Faculty Grants Program.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.460
This program
provides free credits

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.420
to faculty who want to
use GCP in their courses

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.640
with their students.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.170
Courses from computer
science to engineering

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.230
as well as business operations
and anything involving

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.605
data science are already
leveraging GCP credits.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.710
Our second program is the
GCP Research Credits Program.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.320
Researchers can apply
for free credits,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.850
and they can use
this in their lab

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.860
for any type of evaluation,
experimentation, or testing.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.310
We have researchers
sequencing genomes, learning

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.040
and researching about
life forms in galaxies

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.090
far away as well as
far deep underneath--

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.260
not underneath the ocean floor,
but near the ocean floor.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.500
So with that, I would
love to introduce

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.930
our first presentation
from Tomer and Alon

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.780
at Columbia University.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.310
TOMER AHARONI: Imagine not being
able to call your loved ones.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.840
Imagine not being able to
call 911 during an emergency.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.380
Imagine someone was listening
to every single phone

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.370
call you made.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.590
You don't have to imagine.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:11.590
37.5 million adults in
the US report suffering

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.140
from some sort of hearing loss.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.420
That's 15% of the US population.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.250
What do these people do?

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.700
How do they place phone calls?

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.630
Well, today, people with
hearing disabilities

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.480
have to use a
captioning service.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.800
What that means is, if I
want to call a friend who's

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.250
hard of hearing, I'm
being transferred

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.200
to a man in the middle.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.720
That's an actual
human being listening

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.670
to everything I say, type it,
and send it to a special device

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.600
that the other side must own.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.890
Think about it for a second.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.160
It can take almost a
minute from the moment

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.610
I say something to the time
the other side hears it.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:03:59.990
This is crazy.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.120
Let's don't even
talk about privacy.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.400
Someone is listening
to everything I say.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.090
The year is 2019.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.280
We have self-driving cars.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.730
We created rockets that
can go out to space

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.320
and land back on Earth.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.570
And still, if you
have trouble hearing,

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.180
if you're hard of
speaking, there's

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.290
no way for you to make
a decent phone call.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.130
We want to change that.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.375
That's why we created Nagish.

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:38.790
Nagish stands for
"accessible" in Hebrew.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:43.250
And our mission is to make
phone calls more accessible.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.810
How we do that?

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.140
To explain, I'd like to
call my partner, Alon Ezer,

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:48.710
to the stage.

00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:52.110
ALON EZER: All right.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.930
So I want to go back to
the captioning service

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.300
Tomer just mentioned and
tell you a short story that

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.070
happened to me.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.250
So I wanted to contact Jack from
the New York Deaf Association

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.230
to talk about
collaboration, offer him

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:08.570
beta testing for free,
and just hear his opinion.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.210
But I was connected to
a captioning service.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.010
And that was a little
bit strange for me,

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.752
because that was the first
time I really encountered that.

00:05:15.752 --> 00:05:17.210
And by the end of
the conversation,

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.350
it even became more awkward.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.360
I was telling Jack
about this new service

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.790
that Tomer just introduced
that will take away

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.210
the work from the
captioning service,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.830
and I had to do it through
the captioning service.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.290
So it didn't feel right.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.080
And it felt like
there's no privacy.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.880
And I can't even imagine how
Jack feels every single day.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.330
So now that's why
we created Nagish.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.120
We want to bring privacy
and independence back

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.600
to phone calls, so Jack can be
just on the go, use his phone

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.340
whenever he wants.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.925
So how would that conversation
look like today using Nagish?

00:05:54.925 --> 00:05:56.550
Instead of captioning
service and a man

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.950
in the middle that
transcribes everything

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.790
I say back and forth, and
it's slow, tedious process,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.560
we just use Google
Speech-to-Text API,

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.370
which basically takes
my voice, my sound,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.200
and converts it into text.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.880
That text we send to
Jack's mobile phone,

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.440
and now he can just
read what I said.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.370
Simple enough.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.590
Now, if Jack wants
to respond back,

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.003
he can use Google Hangouts,
Facebook Messenger, WhatsApp,

00:06:23.003 --> 00:06:24.420
Telegram, and many
more that we're

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.760
planning to support
in the future,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.610
and just respond, type
back, send a message.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:33.140
When he sends the message,
we convert it back to speech,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.660
to voice, and we
send it to my phone,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.020
and I hear exactly
what Jack typed.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.450
And now we're both happy again.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:47.320
And we have a normal phone
call, like any other person

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:48.370
in the world.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.980
And simple as that.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.480
If this is cool,
the coolest part

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.110
is we were able to build
it in less than 12 hours.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:57.940
We started in a
coding competition

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.860
in our school,
Columbia University,

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.900
and with Google
products, we were

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.896
to have a working demo
by the end of the day.

00:07:04.896 --> 00:07:08.232
And to show you how Nagish
works, here's a video.

00:07:08.232 --> 00:07:08.899
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:07:08.899 --> 00:07:11.344
