00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.940
was generated using the
same technology that

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.743
empowers Nagish.

00:09:28.743 --> 00:09:30.910
ALON EZER: And if you would
like to see a live demo,

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:32.743
please find us that the
Google for Education

00:09:32.743 --> 00:09:34.100
booth right after this talk.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.840
And we're going to be there
until the end of the day.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:37.850
Hope to see you.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:41.828
And now Vaikunth is coming
to speak about space.

00:09:41.828 --> 00:09:42.870
TOMER AHARONI: Thank you.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.060
VAIKUNTH THUKRAL: Thanks, guys.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:48.737
Before I begin, I
just have a question.

00:09:48.737 --> 00:09:51.320
Did any of you get a chance to
check out the latest news today

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:53.400
about the first ever
photo of a black hole?

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.440
It just came out
this morning, right?

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:55.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:55.940
Right.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.400
So, yeah, I'm going to be
talking about similar things

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.030
here.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.830
Hopefully, that
gets you excited.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.100
So, hey, everyone.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.880
My name is Vaikunth,
and I'm a researcher

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.740
at Stanford at
SLAC National Lab,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:08.540
just down the road
from here, and I'd

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.090
like to talk to you about
unlocking a vast trove of data

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.640
to study the secrets of
the universe, cool things

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:15.110
like dark matter
and dark energy.

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.498
96%.

00:10:19.498 --> 00:10:21.040
This might surprise
you, but we don't

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.955
know 96% of what's out
there in the universe.

00:10:23.955 --> 00:10:26.080
Everything you see in that
photo, tens of thousands

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.520
of galaxies, our own galaxy, our
tables, our chairs, you and me,

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:33.370
every single thing
only comprises 4%

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.320
of the matter in the universe.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.140
That's a lot.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:38.432
We don't know what's out there.

00:10:38.432 --> 00:10:40.090
96% is what we need to find out.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:45.680
So to unlock the
secrets of that 96%,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.030
we're going to need
a lot of information.

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:50.800
The universe, just by
virtue of existing,

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.150
provides us with this
information all around us.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.510
We need a way to
collect that information

00:10:55.510 --> 00:11:00.410
and then study it to be able
to study what's going on.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:01.970
This is where I come in.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:03.650
My job as a researcher
is to create

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.590
a system that is able to
collect all this information

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.680
and provide a platform for
astrophysicists and astronomers

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.770
and any scientist who's
interested to study the data

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.090
and look into the
secrets of the universe.

00:11:17.665 --> 00:11:20.290
And with Google, we've also been
able to create a platform that

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.050
helps us do that.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.610
For the data management
system, we're

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.280
going to need a telescope
to power the images that

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.550
give us all that data.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:30.250
This is where LSST comes in.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:33.310
It's called the Large
Synoptic Survey Telescope.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:35.980
This telescope will provide
the deepest, widest image

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.530
of the universe to date.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.482
It's going to be the world's
biggest camera-based telescope.

00:11:40.482 --> 00:11:42.190
Every single picture
that you see online,

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:43.750
even the one that
came out today, it

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:47.500
comes from collaborating with
a bunch of other telescopes,

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.530
taking lots and lots of
exposures, tons of analysis

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:54.040
done on them, lots
of algorithms,

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.830
that eventually gives you a
nice, pretty picture that you

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:58.240
can look at.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.260
Now, how do you do that
for hundreds or thousands,

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.880
and in the end,
millions of pictures?

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.890
We're going to need a
really huge machine.

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:08.170
This is where LSST comes in.

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.853
It's a project being
built in Chile right now.

00:12:10.853 --> 00:12:12.520
And the next video
will show you a sense

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.591
of scale of what this
telescope is about.

00:12:15.591 --> 00:12:16.258
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:12:16.258 --> 00:12:18.743
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:10.920
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:11.420
All right.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:14.373
So, yeah, that should give you
an idea of what's going on.

00:13:14.373 --> 00:13:16.540
It takes hundreds of people,
thousands of scientists

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:17.600
to make this thing.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:20.625
And it's been envisioned
over 10 years ago.

00:13:20.625 --> 00:13:22.510
It finally got funding
in, like, 2010.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.930
And it's been under
construction since.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.910
It's going to be in
production about 2021, '22,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.660
and it'll provide us
with all these images

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:30.200
that we can look at.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.290
So how big is this
telescope exactly?

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:36.678
The camera behind this
telescope is 3,200 megapixels.

00:13:36.678 --> 00:13:37.720
Think of your cell phone.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:39.790
The best ones are usually
10 to 12 megapixels,

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:42.310
and those take
pretty nice photos.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:46.210
Every single exposure with this
telescope is 3,200 megapixels.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:50.410
That's about the size of
40 full moons, one image.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:53.730
It takes one of these
images every 15 seconds,

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:58.300
and it's going to do that over
10 years from 2021 to 2030-ish.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.570
That ends up being 15
terabytes of data per night,

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:07.550
and in the end, 37 billion stars
and galaxies for us to look at.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.550
It's a lot of information
in that database in the end.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:13.000
So with all these hundreds
of scientists, thousands

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.460
of people that are
interested in this data, that

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.550
want to discover new things
from this survey of the sky,

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:20.890
we're going to need a data
management platform that can

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:23.370
easily enable them to do that.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:27.390
So currently, LSST
is based in Chile,

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:30.670
and collaborating with
scientists in the US.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.450
Imagine there's a
scientist in Australia

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:36.390
who's also interested in
studying dark matter and dark

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.450
energy via their own telescope
or their own collaboration,

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.880
and they've never had anything
to do with LSST before.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.860
They have information
about weak lensing,

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.660
for example, which
is one way to study

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:47.838
dark matter and dark energy.

00:14:47.838 --> 00:14:50.380
One of the ways that scientists
make progress on measurements

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:53.260
and equations is to combine
measurements and iterate

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:56.920
on current accepted values.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:00.040
They can bring their own
data, spin up everything

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:02.800
that we've provided in Google
Cloud with our data management

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.550
system, with our services, with
our data compared against what

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:10.060
they have, at their
own cost, which

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:13.840
enables them to combine into a
new measurement for dark matter

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.460
and dark energy.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.310
Cost is, of course, a part
of any equation on campus,

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.950
because that's
really important when

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:22.945
it comes to researchers,
because we always

00:15:22.945 --> 00:15:24.070
rely on government funding.

00:15:27.087 --> 00:15:28.670
This brings us to
the proof of concept

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:31.610
that LSST worked
with with Google.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.820
This was practically an exercise
in running our data management

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:37.970
system that has been planned for
the operation of the telescope

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.160
and running it in the cloud.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.110
This means we took
our database system,

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:46.010
our associated services,
things that we containerized

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:49.420
and Kubernetesized, made it
easily available and deployable

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:54.230
by others, and not just
experts, and run them on GCP.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:55.910
And the way we
compared performance

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:58.770
was by benchmarking
things that we had already

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:02.030
done on our own data centers,
and with things called KPMs

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.250
or key performance metrics.

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.950
This is an ongoing study.

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.850
I cannot really talk too
much about the numbers.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:10.965
But in certain cases,
in certain classes

00:16:10.965 --> 00:16:12.590
of queries that
scientists like to run,

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:16.310
we saw improvements of
up to 10x in the times

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:19.318
that it takes to get
results out of queries.

00:16:19.318 --> 00:16:21.110
One of the things that
I do want to mention

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.735
throughout our proof of concept
is that the Google Professional

00:16:23.735 --> 00:16:26.450
Services people were
really helpful and hands-on

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:28.850
in helping us bring our
entire data management

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:31.527
system onto the cloud, which
really made things easy.

00:16:31.527 --> 00:16:33.110
And in the end, that
is our objective,

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:35.360
to create a system that's
easy, flexible to use,

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.260
and other scientists
and non-experts

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:38.640
should be able to use as well.

00:16:42.455 --> 00:16:43.830
So for other
researchers thinking

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:45.300
about getting on
the cloud, I want

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:46.800
to talk about a
special point, which

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:50.520
is every researcher,
every type of research,

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.093
not just astronomy, not
just biology, and not just--

00:16:54.093 --> 00:16:56.010
literally everything has
special requirements,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.420
because there's very
specific ways to do things.

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:03.990
And it's really bespoke
to the type of analysis

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:04.740
that you're doing.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.400
It's sometimes
difficult to think

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:12.010
that it would be possible to
do that somewhere else, and not

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:13.468
just your own
system, your own data

00:17:13.468 --> 00:17:16.756
center, your own specialized
system that you've created.

00:17:16.756 --> 00:17:18.339
But I wanted to talk
about how we were

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:20.440
able to do that still at GCP.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:23.410
For example, one of the things
that we've done in our system

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:25.315
is something called
shared scans.

00:17:25.315 --> 00:17:27.067
And what that does
is, when you submit

00:17:27.067 --> 00:17:28.900
a query that looks at
the entire data, which

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:31.570
is a very frequent thing
that scientists like to do,

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:35.160
any further query that comes
into the system tags along,

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.480
and it adds no further
overhead to the system.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.300
And that way we can
look at the entire data

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.380
sets for one query
or 100 queries,

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:44.110
and it should work
exactly the same.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:47.050
Another specific thing that
astronomers need for LSST

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:50.008
is something called
spherical geometry.

00:17:50.008 --> 00:17:51.550
Think about Google
Maps for a second.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.160
When you kind of scroll out,
you see that the coordinates

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:55.782
are in a sphere, right?

00:17:55.782 --> 00:17:58.240
You zoom out, and you look at
distances between two points,

00:17:58.240 --> 00:17:59.523
it's actually a curve.

00:17:59.523 --> 00:18:00.940
In the same way,
when we're taking

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.070
photos of the night sky
and all these images,

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:06.290
if you think about it, we're
in the center of that globe.

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:09.190
So we need an accurate
way to look for objects,

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:13.326
around other objects, distances
between things accurately

00:18:13.326 --> 00:18:15.880
with spherical geometry in
mind, so we can calculate

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:17.593
those things accurately.

00:18:17.593 --> 00:18:19.510
Things like that were
also adaptable in things

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:20.980
like BigQuery,
where we could make

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:24.070
special UDFs and other
functions to be able to do

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:25.600
that in the way that we need.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:31.630
So I can go on and on about
all the specific things

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.610
that we did and tried.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:36.430
But if you're interested more
about the LSST data management

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:40.270
team, you can find
out more in that link.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.040
And ultimately,
what I want to say

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.550
is that our objective is to
build a system that is really

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:48.250
easy to use, flexible
for other scientists,

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:51.760
for other non-experts,
and really

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.580
enables science output out
of this experiment for as

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.900
many papers as possible, and
have the scientists concentrate

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:01.240
more on delivering
science results

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:04.510
rather than worrying about
the underlying technology,

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:06.940
because after all, we still
have 96% of the universe left

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:08.440
to understand.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.192
With that, I will hand over
to the next speaker, Nil,

00:19:11.192 --> 00:19:13.150
who would like to talk
about the opioid crisis.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:14.647
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:19.637 --> 00:19:22.340
NILKANTH PATEL:
Thank you, Vaikunth.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:23.640
Hi, everyone.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.220
My name is Nilkanth
Patel, and I am

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:27.020
a graduate student
at the University

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:28.830
of Southern California.

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:32.750
And today I'm going to
share a story about fight

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:36.950
against the opioid epidemic
in the Google Cloud.

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:40.780
What we are trying to do here
is to design better drug, better

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.250
painkillers faster.

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:46.370
What takes more than a
year in a normal research

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:50.860
lab takes less than a
day on Google Cloud.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.280
Opioid epidemic.

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:56.290
So the US is currently facing
a true national emergency

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:57.730
as opioid epidemic.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:00.610
And more than 130
people every day,

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:02.620
or close to 50,000
people every year,

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:05.290
die because of opioid epidemic.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:07.420
To put this in
perspective, this number

00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:11.740
is even higher than people
dying from car accidents.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:15.260
In fact, in the next 10
minutes, while I do this talk,

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.100
one person will die because
of opioids in the US.

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:25.990
So let's look at
where this started.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:29.980
The use of opioids dates
back to even centuries.

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:32.350
And its social and
cultural impacts

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:34.880
can still be found in
early civilizations,

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:37.600
including Romans
and the Egyptians.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.230
Back then, it was all derived
from natural products.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:43.210
It was derived from poppies.

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:48.740
But now, as you can see here,
it has gone totally synthetic.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:52.281
It has become much more powerful
and much more addictive.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:58.610
Back then, it had
impacted human history,

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:01.790
and wars were fought
because of opioids.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:04.970
One such example is
the first Opium War,

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:07.220
which was fought between
British-backed East India

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:10.280
Company and the Chinese kingdom.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:13.700
Back then, we only had morphine,
which is derived from poppy.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:17.660
But with even more
powerful synthetic opioids,

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:21.980
nowadays, the challenges
become even more grave.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:24.530
And take a look at
the map of the US

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:28.640
and see how it's impacting the
US at every level of income,

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:31.380
culture, and race.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:33.960
Take a moment and find
the region you belong to

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:35.430
and see how severely
it's impacted

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:36.946
by the opioid epidemic.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.330
So let me switch
gears and see how

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:45.983
this works in terms of biology.

00:21:45.983 --> 00:21:47.400
So in layman's
term, you can think

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:50.400
of these opioid
drugs as keys, keys

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.680
which binds to a set of
proteins in our brain

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:56.230
called the opioid receptors.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.180
So human body is
made up of cells.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.930
And cells have cell
walls engulfing them.

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:05.290
And the cell membranes
have these gatekeepers

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:10.070
or these opioid receptors
sitting across the membrane.

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.430
And when these keys
or extra molecules

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:14.530
bind to these
receptors, either they

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:18.760
activate them or
they block them.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.370
And when it comes to pain
management, in the brain,

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.730
there are two major pathways.

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:25.890
One pathway leads
to pain relief,

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.570
which is the desired effect.

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.820
But activating
another pathway leads

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:32.910
to undesired side effects,
such as respiratory depression

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:36.510
and tolerance.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:40.320
So whether drug will activate
one pathway or another

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.590
or it will block
the receptor, it

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:46.110
depends on the structure
of these keys or the drugs

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:50.700
and how they interact
with these receptors.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.090
So you don't really have
a choice as to whether you

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.620
want to get addicted or not.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:56.490
It's not in our hands.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.463
It's up to these
receptors and how

00:22:58.463 --> 00:22:59.630
they function in our brains.

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:04.817
So here is a short
visualization.

00:23:04.817 --> 00:23:06.650
It's actually a molecular
dynamic simulation

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:10.180
showing an opioid drug
binding to an opioid receptor.

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:13.598
So in purple, this drug
and the blue spirals

00:23:13.598 --> 00:23:14.390
is opioid receptor.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:21.390
So an answer to fight this
challenge of opioid epidemic

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:24.870
is redesign and
find better keys,

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:27.180
keys that bind these receptors.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.940
And our lab has been
working on these targets

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.200
since more than a decade.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.640
So here is what we are
going to do to fight this.

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:42.290
We and our colleagues have
solved the atomic-level 3D

00:23:42.290 --> 00:23:45.710
structures of these
receptors or these locks.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:47.910
And once you know
how these locks work,

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:50.870
then you can design
and screen better keys.

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:53.810
So once we have the description
or two-dimensional description

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:55.790
of these receptors,
then we throw

00:23:55.790 --> 00:24:00.080
in bunch of keys or
small druglike molecules,

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:03.320
and find those ones
which can interact

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:06.640
with these keys, these locks,
and show desired effect.

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:11.990
So here is where the
action happens, actually.

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:16.210
So in this video, it
may look like a bunch

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:17.730
of things happening.

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:21.970
What it's actually showing is
we are screening different keys.

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:26.110
And this process is called
virtual [INAUDIBLE] screening.

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:28.810
In this process, we
evaluate a single drug

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:31.600
molecule in different
blocking poses,

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:35.530
and we calculate different
chemical interactions for them.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:39.140
And through this
process, there are

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:41.120
thousands of
different orientations

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:43.630
which are evaluated
for every single drug.

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:45.980
And this process happens
for hundreds of millions

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.650
of druglike compounds.

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.590
So you might be wondering,
why is this important?

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:54.910
So by this process, we
can find most promising

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:58.690
druglike candidates, and then
we can select them and test them

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.680
actually in the lab.

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.710
And here the story gets
even more interesting.

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:06.510
In the past two
years, the size of

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:11.440
these small druglike molecule
libraries have grown rapidly.

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:15.240
What used to be in tens of
millions a few years ago is now

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:20.170
have grown to more
than a billion in size.

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:24.120
So while this presents
a great challenge

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.150
in terms of size of the
problem and compatibility,

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:28.920
this presents a
unique opportunity

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:32.100
in terms of exploring the
ever-expanding chemical

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:36.300
space to have more options
to find better druglike

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:37.110
chemicals from.

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:41.360
So in a nutshell,
what's worked for

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:47.310
us is the speed at which we can
do computations on the cloud.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.410
So let me give you
a simple comparison.

00:25:50.410 --> 00:25:53.410
What we have here in
our lab is on the left--

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:55.840
it's usually 500 processors--

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:58.370
and what we have on the cloud.

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:02.510
So you are using almost
200,000 processors in parallel

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:04.620
at a given point in time.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.400
And what used to
take more than a

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:11.450
year to screen at such a scale
now takes less than a day

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:12.740
or two on the cloud.

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:20.720
So far in this story, which
began almost a year ago,

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:22.910
with the help of National
Institute of Health

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:27.740
and its pilot program
on cloud computing,

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:31.285
and also with the
help from NIDA,

00:26:31.285 --> 00:26:32.660
National Institute
of Drug Abuse,

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:37.540
and Google Research
Credit Programs,

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.610
we screened almost
half a billion

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:43.400
of such druglike compounds.

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:48.410
And we selected 120 compounds
and tested them in the lab.

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.340
And indeed we found some of
the promising compounds, which

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:57.660
have shown desired
signaling profiles.

00:26:57.660 --> 00:27:00.900
So we'll continue our fight
against the opioid epidemic

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:05.160
in the cloud in the hope
of saving millions of lives

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.010
and changing the faces
of the addiction.

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:09.845
With that, I'd like
to thank you all.

00:27:09.845 --> 00:27:14.410
And special thanks to my mentor,
Professor Vsevolod Katritch,

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:18.790
and Sarosh and Edward from
Google, and Jim from Onix.

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:20.460
ALICIA SALMERON:
Tomer and Alon, I

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:21.835
thought it was
fantastic that you

00:27:21.835 --> 00:27:25.590
were able to get going
with a prototype in one

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:29.225
day at a campus hackathon.

00:27:29.225 --> 00:27:30.600
What would the
advice be that you

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:32.340
would give to other
students that are

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:35.840
looking to get started on GCP?

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:39.470
TOMER AHARONI: So when
I got started with GCP,

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:42.530
I didn't have much
programming experience.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.290
I took a few classes,
but not more than that.

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:48.620
And what I would say is
just go and try stuff.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.530
I failed my first hackathon.

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:54.020
And we won the second
one and I think every one

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:55.970
that we went to since then.

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:57.110
So just keep trying.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:27:58.250
Do crazy stuff.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:02.820
Do easy stuff, then go
to stuff that are harder,

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:03.620
and keep trying.

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:07.010
The GCP console makes it really
easy to achieve great stuff.

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:13.430
ALON EZER: I think Tomer
covered most of it.

00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:16.130
Like he said, begin
